{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "Getting It Right", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Coming to a Compromise", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 9, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 18, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "B2.2", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Describe necessity, duty, advice, or strong disapproval in written and spoken situations according to work and social contexts.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_1"}, {"content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_2"}, {"content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_3"}, {"content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_4"}, {"content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_5"}, {"content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"content": "engenn10le18ob09re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Wh-clause with Prepositions", "type": "heading"}, {"content": " You can use wh-clauses with prepositions.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Even though not all verbs require a preposition, you can form wh-clauses after prepositions.<br/> <span class=\"italic\"><ul><li>I <span class=\"underline\">blamed</span> my boyfriend <span class=\"underline\">for</span> <span class=\"strong\">why</span> we were late. <li>My father <span class=\"underline\">replied to</span> <span class=\"strong\">what</span> my mother said.</li></ul></span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "If a verb in the wh-clause needs a preposition, it can come at the end of the wh-clause. In more formal language, the preposition can come before the wh-clause. <span class=\"italic\"><ul><li>The doctor didn't know <span class=\"strong\">what</span> the patient was <span class=\"underline\">suffering from</span>. <li>The doctor didn't know <span class=\"underline\">from</span> what the patient was <span class=\"underline\">suffering</span>.</li></ul></span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\"><ul><li>My mother never asked <span class=\"strong\">which</span> school club <span class=\"underline\">I belong to</span>. <li>My mother never asked <span class=\"underline\">to</span> <span class=\"strong\">which</span> school club I <span class=\"underline\">belong</span>.</li></ul></span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You may be wondering where I got this 'situation' from. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "To prepare for this speech, I consulted my business partner on why compromising is important. <br/> <br/>", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": " Willingness, Refusals, Promises and Threats", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You can use <span class=\"italic\">will </span> and <span class=\"italic\">would </span> to show willingness or refusal to do something, and make promises and threats.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Look at the sentences below. What do you think they mean?", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\">I <span class=\"strong\">will not wake up</span> early when I'm on vacation.<br/> My friend <span class=\"strong\">won't</span> ever <span class=\"strong\">eat</span> at a restaurant alone.<br/> Skype <span class=\"strong\">won't work</span> without an Internet connection.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">Will</span> you <span class=\"strong\">help</span> me with my bags, please?<br/> <span class=\"strong\">Would</span> you <span class=\"strong\">give</span> him a message for me?</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\">I <span class=\"strong\">will try</span> my best to help you pass your exam.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\">If you don't do this favor for me, I <span class=\"strong\">will tell</span> your mother that you stole $50 from her last week. </span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"prelo-letter-page-break\"></span>Look at the explanations below. Did you understand them correctly?", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You use <span class=\"strong\">will not</span> or <span class=\"strong\">won't</span> to show that you don't want to do something. You are unwilling to do something and you refuse. Other people and things can also refuse. <ul><li><span class=\"italic\">I <span class=\"strong\">will not wake up</span> early when I'm on vacation.</span> (I refuse to wake up early when I'm on vacation.) <li><span class=\"italic\">My friend <span class=\"strong\">won't</span> ever <span class=\"strong\">eat</span> at a restaurant alone.</span> (My friend refuses to eat at a restaurant alone.) <li><span class=\"italic\">Skype <span class=\"strong\">won't work</span> without an Internet connection.</span> (Skype refuses to work without an Internet connection.)</li></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "If you want to ask someone if they are willing to do something, you can use <span class=\"strong\">will</span> or <span class=\"strong\">would</span>. <ul><li><span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">Will</span> you <span class=\"strong\">help</span> me with my bags, please?</span> (Are you willing to help me with my bags?) <li><span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">Would</span> you <span class=\"strong\">give</span> him a message for me?</span> (Are you willing to give him a message for me?)</li></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You can also use <span class=\"strong\">will</span> to make a promise to do something in the future. <ul><li><span class=\"italic\">I <span class=\"strong\">will do</span> my best to help you pass your exam.</span> (I promise to do my best to help you pass your exam.)</li></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">Will</span> can also signal a threat if a promise has a negative result.  <ul><li><span class=\"italic\">If you don't do this favor for me, I <span class=\"strong\">will tell</span> your mother that you stole $50 from her last week. </span>(Something bad will happen if you don't do this favor for me.)</li></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "If you will allow me, I'd like to start with a scenario.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "You might say to yourself: \u201cI won't make any changes!\u201d or \u201cIf this goes on, I will abandon the project!\u201d ", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "stand your ground", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to resist something; be consistent with what you believe in", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Compromising can be difficult. It makes you feel you have lost. It's important to know when to stand your ground.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le18ob09aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you use when you negotiate a compromise with someone.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "cooperation", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the action of cooperating (getting along) with other people", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: In my view, your ability to get things done at work almost always depends on the cooperation of other people.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le18ob09aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "negotiator", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a person who negotiates or bargains to reach an agreement, such as a contract, with another person ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The best negotiators start by saying something positive. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le18ob09aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "I can't believe it", "type": "name"}, {"content": "this is crazy; unbelievable", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example:  You may think: \u201cI can't believe it!  This really makes me mad!\u201d ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le18ob09aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "choose your battles", "type": "name"}, {"content": "select some things to fight for but ignore other things", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: It's important to know how to choose your battles at work and at home.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le18ob09aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "With a partner, discuss situations that require two people to make a compromise.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Select one situation.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write a dialogue of 12 - 15 lines. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Use expressions from this lesson, such as <span class=\"italic\">stand your ground</span> and <span class=\"italic\">choose your battles</span>.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Practice your dialogue.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Change roles.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I write a dialogue of 12 - 15 lines.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I give suggestions for making a compromise.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I respond to suggestions by giving my opinion.", "type": "text"}]}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"_data": [{"type": "lab_options", "contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}]}], "type": "scholar", "id": 0, "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "img_path": "", "id": 0, "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": " I can talk about beliefs, and opinions, and express annoyance.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can report details, reasons, or results of events or actions. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can express willingness and refusal, and make promises and threats.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " Are you good at making a compromise? Why or why not?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": " What makes it hard to compromise?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": " Do you think compromising is important sometimes? Why or why not?", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}], "allo